# A prompt whose client left stops at its next chunk instead of filling to its end (TF_GLM_PROMPT_STOP):
#
# - Why: a single-stream prompt ran every chunk to the end before a reply's stop vote (0070's StopVote, one round after
#   the first token) could end the request, so a dropped long prompt held both ranks for the whole prefill. Under
#   --parallel only sliced fills (TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS>0) noticed a client that left while its prompt filled.
# - Single stream: decode.PromptStop (this rank's wish: rank 0 polls the request's `cancelled`, other ranks never wish)
#   and decode.PromptStopped. After each prompt chunk but the last, prefill() asks the vote: every rank all-gathers its
#   wish as one int32 (one tiny collective a chunk, a chunk runs for seconds) and all stop when any rank wishes, so all
#   ranks stop after the same chunk. Engine.prompt_stop carries it; GlmEngine._run_once sets it for the request
#   (rank 0's `on_tokens.cancelled`), catches PromptStopped, leaves `live` as the prompt's rows before the stop, remembers
#   the states kept on the way and reports `prompt_stopped`; generate() then raises RequestCancelled.
# - decode.prompt_stop_on: TF_GLM_PROMPT_STOP = 1 (default) or 0 (every prompt fills to its end); other values are an
#   error. It joins the ranks' startup comparison (the vote is a collective: ranks with different settings would hang).
# - Multi-stream (--parallel): MultiDecoder._iterate polls every filling stream's `cancelled` before its next chunk, not
#   only with TF_GLM_FILL_BUDGET_MS>0 (the sliced path already did): a stream whose client left ends at once; rank 0
#   polls, the FINISH it sends makes both ranks drop it.
#
# What Flash already had: the sliced-fill cancellation poll in _iterate (kept; now unconditional), queued-request
# cancellation in the scheduler (_cancel_waiting), the reply's StopVote in decode, and `feed.cancelled` on the single
# stream's on_tokens (cuda/server.py). It had no stop inside a prompt on the single-stream path.
#
# Bits: a stop only ends the prefill after a whole chunk, so the committed rows are those of a prefix run of the
# prompt in the same chunking, and a later prompt that extends it resumes from them as from any prefix state. Without a
# wish, or with TF_GLM_PROMPT_STOP=0, every chunk runs as before (the vote reads no model state).
#
# Tests (CPU, tests/cpu/test_prompt_stop.py in the recipe): the setting; the vote across 2 / 3 / 4 ranks as threads
# (any rank's wish, a broken poll never wishes, sticky); all ranks stop after the same chunk; committed rows equal a
# prefix run's and resume a longer prompt exactly; no stop without a wish, on the last chunk or with the setting off;
# _run_once and generate() report the stop and raise RequestCancelled; the startup comparison; and a real tiny model
# on two gloo ranks (the chunk forward in Triton's interpreter) whose stopped caches equal a prefix run's bit for bit.
# The scheduler half is in test_multi_quick_fills.py (a filling stream whose client left ends before its next chunk,
# with and without sliced fills).
#
# Provenance and credit: ported from the GLM-5.3 recipe's patch 0168 (Mia's AI Lab), after upstream TensorFold's prompt
# stop at chunk boundaries by Ash Hart (ashhart), commit 05dfb7b, Apache 2.0. The multi-rank prompt vote and the
# scheduler's cancelled fills are our code.
